(a) Generally. Authorization cards, obtainable at city hall will be issued to licensed repair personnel who have been called to the area to make emergency repairs to buildings. Persons other than residents of the emergency site, government staff and health care professionals may enter only with a valid authorization card. Personnel from the utility companies and private repair personnel who have been called to the area for a specific purpose by a specific resident or business in the area shall be excluded unless they possess an authorization card. Unlicensed repair personnel who have not been requested to come to the area shall be directed to the EOC for instructions and information about obtaining a business license and an authorization card. These restrictions will remain in effect until the emergency is terminated by expiration or by order of the mayor, or city manager.
(b) Application. When registering, any person, partnership, corporation or other entity making application must, under oath, complete an application, providing the following information:
(1) Name of applicant;
(2) Permanent address and phone number of applicant;
(3) Applicant's Social Security number or federal employer identification number;
(4) If applicant is a corporation, the state and date of incorporation;
(5) Tag registration information for each vehicle to be used in the business;
(6) List of cities and/or counties where the applicant has conducted business within the past 12 months;
(7) Georgia sales tax number or authorization;
(8) Georgia business license number, if required.
(c) Gouging prohibited.
(1) Generally. To preserve, protect or sustain the life, health or safety of persons or property within a designated area upon the declaration of emergency or disaster, it shall be unlawful, during the duration of the emergency or subsequent recovery period, for any person, firm or corporation located or doing business within the city limits of Douglasville too overcharge for any goods, materials, foods, equipment, supplies, services, labor, motel rooms, temporary lodging or houses sold or rented within Douglasville.
(2) Defined. Overcharging means charging prices for goods, materials, foods, equipment, supplies, services, labor, motel rooms, temporary lodging or houses, which exceed the customary charges by at least 25 percent or, in applicable cases, which exceed by at least 25 percent the suppliers' or providers' costs for such goods, materials, foods, equipment, supplies, services, labor, motel rooms, temporary lodging or houses. The existence of overcharging shall be presumed from a 25 percent or greater increase in the price at which the merchandise or rate, fee or cost/rental of housing was offered in the usual course of business immediately prior to the onset of the emergency or disaster, but shall not include increases in cost to the supplier directly attributable to the higher cost of material, supplies and labor costs resulting from the emergency.
(Ord. No. O-2015-15, § 1(Exh. A), 4-20-15)

