(5) To delegate any of his or her functions and powers under this article to such officers and agents as he or she may designate.
(Ord. No. O-04-46, § 1, 10-18-04)

741:
The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building, or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use if he/she finds that conditions exist in such building, dwelling, or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building, or structure, of the occupants of neighborhood dwelling, buildings, or structures; or of other residents or visitors to the City of Douglasville. Such conditions include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing).
(1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents or other calamities;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation;
(4) Disrepair;
(5) Structural defects;
(6) Uncleanliness; and
(7) Other additional standards which may from time to time by adopted and referenced herein by ordinance amendment.
The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building, or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes based upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed.
(Ord. No. O-04-46, § 1, 10-18-04)

769:
(a) The smoking prohibition shall not apply in the following areas:
(1) Any restaurant to which access is denied at all times and on all days to any person under the age of 18 and that does not employ at any time and on any day any individual under the age of 18, provided that smoking is allowed only within the designated bar area;
(2) Any retail tobacco store provided that secondhand smoke from such stores does not infiltrate into areas where smoking is prohibited;
(3) Any guest room for sleeping and the adjoining suite areas of any hotels, motels and extended-stay residences rented for 14 days or less, provided that not more than 20 percent of the rooms rented to guests are designated as smoking rooms;
(4) Any private residence, including a private residence which may serve as an office workplace (except if used as a children's daycare, an adult day care or health care facility);
(5) Any private or semiprivate room in health care facilities licensed under Title 31 [of the Official Code of Georgia] that are occupied by one or more persons, all of whom have written authorization by their treating physician to smoke.
(6) Any outdoor area of any place of employment.
(b) Except for private residences, any area listed above shall post conspicuously at every entrance a sign indicating that smoking is permitted.
(Ord. No. O-04-46, § 1, 10-18-04; Ord. No. O-05-25, § 1, 6-6-05; Ord. No. O-06-32, § 1, 9-18-06)

